.TH CHMOD 1 "September 19, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
chmod \- change file access
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B chmod
.I +flags file ...
.br
.B chmod
.I \-flags file ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
The mode of each file is changed.
Unlike Research Unix,
.I chmod
uses ProDOS access flags, not octal modes or owner/group/other permissions.
A leading + sets the named bits; a leading - clears them.
.TP
.B r
Read enabled.
.TP
.B w
Write enabled.
.TP
.B d
Destroy enabled.
.TP
.B n
Rename enabled.
.TP
.B b
Backup needed.
.PP
For example,
.B chmod \-dnw file
locks a file against deletion, renaming and writing;
.B chmod +dnw file
unlocks it again.
Unmentioned bits, file type, auxiliary type and timestamps are preserved.
A failed operand is reported and the remaining files are attempted.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/CHMOD  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
ls(1), licenses(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0 if every file succeeds, 1 for access/change failures, 2 for invalid flags or missing operands.
.SH BUGS
Unix rwx and numeric modes are not supported. The leading + or - is required.
